From ee54aa427fe4a4fed270eae9f48b0ecffe27c8a2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: direntry <312071520+direntry@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 12:38:19 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Add a streaming writer with an up-front layout plan

An EROFS image can only be produced with a seekable destination today:
Create lays the image out data-first and seeks back to write the real
superblock once the metadata sizes are known. That rules out writing an
image straight into a pipe, a compressor or a hash, and it gives the
caller no way to learn where a file's payload will land before the bytes
are gone.

Add a second finalizer that does both. NewWriter builds the same tree but
has no output attached; Prepare freezes it, plans the metadata-first
layout and returns it; WriteTo emits exactly that layout in ascending
offset order and needs no seeking. assignDataBlocks already supported a
metadata-first layout, so this mostly factors the plan/serialize split
out of Close.

The returned Layout describes the out-of-line payloads as extents: for a
regular file the range holds its bytes verbatim, followed by zero padding
to the next block, so a caller can address a file's content inside the
image without parsing it back. SetToken lets it carry per-entry context
(a precomputed digest, say) through to the matching extent.

Alongside that, the pieces a content-addressed producer needs:

  - Link adds a second dirent for an existing inode, with nlink counted.
  - ShareData points one inode's payload at another's extent, keeping the
    two inodes' metadata independent, so duplicate content is stored once.
    A shared payload is never tail-packed, since an inline payload has no
    extent to share.
  - WithInlineThreshold caps tail-packing so the caller decides which
    files get an extent of their own.
  - WithSyntheticDirMetadata and SyntheticDirs deal with the directories a
    filesystem image needs but a layer changeset need not mention: the
    hook gives them meaningful attributes (inheriting from the nearest
    ancestor, as mkfs.erofs does), and the report names the ones that were
    never described explicitly so a caller can warn or fail.
  - Remove deletes an entry, which is the primitive behind
    last-writer-wins replacement. It refuses to dangle a hardlink or a
    shared payload.
  - WithUUID sets the superblock UUID.

Mkdir now updates an existing directory in place instead of failing,
which is what makes it possible to describe a directory after something
beneath it, and is also what clears the synthesized flag.

Signed-off-by: direntry <312071520+direntry@users.noreply.github.com>
